Summary verse (uddāna):

Meghiya, Uddhatā, Gopāla, Yakkha, and with Nāga as the fifth;
Piṇḍola and Sāriputta, Sundarī is the eighth;
Upasena, Vaṅkantaputta, and Sāriputta — these are the ten.

Footnote 1: Juṇhā — Sī(haḷa edition), Syā(m edition), Ro(man edition).

3. The Discourse on Well-Spoken Words

Thus have I heard. On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Savatthi, in the Jeta Grove, in the monastery of Anathapindika. There the Blessed One addressed the monks: "Monks!" "Venerable Sir," those monks replied to the Blessed One. The Blessed One said this:

"Monks, speech endowed with four qualities is well-spoken, not ill-spoken, blameless and not faulted by the wise. What are the four? Here, monks, a monk speaks only what is well-spoken, not what is ill-spoken; speaks only the Dhamma, not what is contrary to the Dhamma; speaks only what is pleasant, not what is unpleasant; speaks only what is true, not what is false. Speech endowed with these four qualities, monks, is well-spoken, not ill-spoken, blameless and not faulted by the wise." This is what the Blessed One said. Having said this, the Fortunate One, the Teacher, further said:

46. "The good declare well-spoken speech to be the highest;
      speak the Dhamma, not what is contrary — that is the second;
      speak what is pleasant, not unpleasant — that is the third;
      speak truth, not falsehood — that is the fourth."

Then the Venerable Vangisa rose from his seat, arranged his robe over one shoulder, paid homage toward the Blessed One with joined palms, and said: "It occurs to me, Blessed One, it occurs to me, Fortunate One." "Let it occur to you, Vangisa," said the Blessed One. Then the Venerable Vangisa praised the Blessed One face to face with fitting verses:

47. "One should speak only that word by which one does not torment oneself,
      and by which one does not harm others — that indeed is well-spoken speech.

48. "One should speak only pleasant words, words that are welcomed;
      those words that one speaks pleasantly, not taking up evil things for others,

